<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashoka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 232 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ashoka was a Maurya emperor who ruled most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> in the third century BCE and died in 232 BCE. After his bloody conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> he embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and promoted a policy of dhamma, publicised through inscriptions on rocks and pillars. His reign marked the peak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>, which declined soon after his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashoka.jpg" alt="Ashoka" width="320" height="305" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo Dharma from Sadao, Thailand · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashoka%27s_visit_to_the_Ramagrama_stupa_Sanchi_Stupa_1_Southern_gateway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Pandyas were one of the three ancient Tamil royal lineages, named with the Cholas and the Cheras in the Sangam poems and in <b>Ashoka</b>’s inscriptions, and associated with Madurai on the Vaigai river.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Later Pandyan Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ashoka</b>'s Lion Capital from Sarnath, carved from polished sandstone over 2,000 years ago, is today the national emblem of the Republic of India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…empress converted to Buddhism to justify imperial authority and the conquest of Chen; the emperor presented himself as a Cakravartin king who would defend the faith by force, and in 601 distributed relics of the Buddha to temples throughout China, an imitation of <b>Ashoka</b> of the Maurya Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Buddhism’s first great patron was <b>Ashoka</b>, the Mauryan emperor of the 3rd century BCE, who after his bloody conquest of Kalinga turned to the Buddha’s teaching.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The Indian emperor <b>Ashoka</b>, in the 3rd century BCE, addressed the peoples of his north-western frontier in Aramaic, as in his bilingual inscription at Kandahar.</blockquote><a href="/language/aramaic">Aramaic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>K. R. Norman suggests that around the time of <b>Ashoka</b> an attempt was made to gather all the teachings, and that a language close to the Pali of the canon may have emerged then as a compromise among those dialects.</blockquote><a href="/language/pali">Pali · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.61,85.14&amp;year=-242" aria-label="Patna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6803 1222 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ashoka"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7253 1492h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7253 1492h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.61,85.14&amp;year=-242">What was here in 242 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For he who does reverence to his own sect while disparaging the sects of others wholly from attachment to his own, with intent to enhance the splendour of his own sect, in reality by such conduct inflicts the severest injury on his own sect.&#8221;</p><footer>Rock Edict XII &#183; tr. Vincent A. Smith, 1920 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Asoka_-_the_Buddhist_Emperor_of_India/Chapter_4" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;And whatsoever efforts I make they are made that I may attain release from my debt to animate beings, so that while in this world I make some persons happy, they may win heaven in the world beyond.&#8221;</p><footer>Rock Edict VI &#183; tr. Vincent A. Smith, 1920 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Asoka_-_the_Buddhist_Emperor_of_India/Chapter_4" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Father and mother must be hearkened to; similarly, respect for living creatures must be firmly established; truth must be spoken. These are the virtues of the Law of Piety which must be practiced.&#8221;</p><footer>Minor Rock Edict II &#183; tr. Vincent A. Smith (in E. M. Tappan, The World's Story, 1914) &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/india/ashoka-edicts.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Formerly, in the kitchen of His Sacred and Gracious Majesty the King each day many hundred thousands of living creatures were slaughtered to make curries.&#8221;</p><footer>Rock Edict I &#183; tr. Vincent A. Smith (in E. M. Tappan, The World's Story, 1914) &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/india/ashoka-edicts.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For the welfare of all folk is what I must work for---and the root of that, again, is in effort and the dispatch of business.&#8221;</p><footer>Rock Edict VI &#183; tr. Vincent A. Smith (in E. M. Tappan, The World's Story, 1914) &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/india/ashoka-edicts.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Thence arises the remorse of His Sacred Majesty for having conquered the Kalingas, because the conquest of a country previously unconquered involves the slaughter, death, and carrying away captive of the people.&#8221;</p><footer>Rock Edict XIII, on the conquest of Kalinga &#183; tr. Vincent A. Smith, 1920 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Asoka_-_the_Buddhist_Emperor_of_India/Chapter_4" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
